  "notes": "{為|な}す is a literary/formal verb meaning 'to accomplish' or 'to do.' In modern speech, する is much more common.\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Type: Transitive ({他動詞|たどうし})\n- Pattern: Xを{為|な}す\n\nREGISTER: Literary/Formal\nThis verb is primarily used in formal writing, set expressions, and proverbs. It's rarely used in everyday conversation.\n\nCOMMON EXPRESSIONS:\n- {為|な}すべきこと (what should be done)\n- {為|な}す{術|すべ}がない (to have no way to do anything)\n- {志|こころざし}を{為|な}す (to accomplish one's aspiration)\n- 「{為|な}せば{成|な}る」(Where there's a will, there's a way)\n\nNOTE: Often written in hiragana as なす or in set expressions.",
